ver  experienced love or compassion she's  only ever been used and violated and so  that's pretty much literally the only  experience that she's ever had of the  world and the people in it  now she does think of her older brother  lim as her protector which he  technically is although lim as a person  almost kind of seems like a blank slate  it seems as though he is someone who  because he is you know  involved in the same situation obviously  and you're led to believe that he has  been abused as well  um so his kind of way of dealing with it  it seems is to completely disconnect  from all of his emotions other than rage  perhaps um in order to cope with what's  going on  or it's you know it could be that he  never had those emotions in the first  place um you know so it could be either  one of those things  uh but his instincts toward his sister  do appear to be those of like uh of a  fierce guardian type of thing  um and he will basically you know maim  or kill anyone who simply looks at her  or talks about her the wrong way  now susie makes it pretty clear on  several occasions during the narrative  that she  admires her brother a great deal uh one  because everyone is terrified of him  because he's really really scary  and two because he almost seems  invincible like she perceives him as  invincible which i guess would be a  common thing for  uh you know a brother-sister kind of  dynamic in this way like in growing up  in this abusive household like i'm  assuming that that's kind of how you  would perceive your older brother now  during one particularly distressing  uh session of abuse at the hands of her  mother susie finally snaps and uh beats  the woman to death  after which lim suggests that they set  the house on fire and skip town  now it should be mentioned here that at  some point in the recent past  mama had kidnapped a young girl named  alice  and kept her chained in the basement  now susie who had sometimes i guess been  comforted by talking to the captive  alice like through this air vent in her  room  briefly considers rescuing the kid from  the flames  but then  shrugs that impulse off and so her and  lim simply leave alice there to burn to  death with really nothing but the barest  twinge of regret  so as should be apparent from that last  sentence that i just said  susie as a character is frighteningly  sociopathic  but the thing that makes her  terrifying  is that she isn't portrayed the way  sociopaths are often portrayed in  fiction that's why i kind of think that  comparisons of this book like to natural  born killers are maybe a little bit off  the mark  like you know in natural born killers  mickey and mallory seem to kind of like  revel in their cruelty and their  sociopathy and they're represented that  way in the movie because the movie was  trying to make a statement about how the  media sometimes tends to romanticize or  glamorize people like that to some  extent susie on the other hand is kind  of a different kind of animal altogether  she doesn't necessarily seem to enjoy  violence in the strictest definition of  the word more in line with a true  sociopath i think she doesn't really  seem to enjoy much of anything  since all of her emotions it seemed have  been kind of like dulled down and numbed  by years of traumatic abuse she's  basically like a hollow shell of a  person  who sees other human beings particularly  women as things they're just a  collection of parts to be used as needed  and discarded um and it's obvious too  that she thinks of herself in this same  way  um she sometimes contemplates like  cutting off her breasts or like scooping  out her own genitalia she doesn't really  seem to have any fear  of physical pain for herself or death  for herself she just seems  almost just very cold like a little bit  indifferent like i said as you would be  like somebody that had experienced what  she experienced now she like she'll  occasionally comment for example that  she finds a woman beautiful  but in a way  that makes her want to take that woman  apart and see what's inside you know  what i mean she also seems to have  really no use for men in general except  for her brother she pretty much  perceives them all as predators uh that  can be again like used and killed as  necessary so after susie and lim like  flee their home after burning it down  they're on the run for a bit uh going on  something of like a mini killing spree  now although again many people compared  this segment of the book to natural born  killers the tone isn't really the same  at all uh yes they do kill people  usually for  this is gonna sound like weird but  usually for kind of like practical  reasons at least at first like they need  to steal money or they need to steal a  car or they need a place to stay or  something like that now occasionally  they do indulge in what seems to be a  murderous whim but they aren't exactly  like partying about it if that makes any  sense you know what i mean like if you  think of  you know like  in other words these aren't like these  kind of gleefully vicious like rob  zombie characters you know what i mean  like it's not like a baby from you know  like house of a thousand corpses or uh  you know devil's rejects or anything  like that where they're having like a  good time and they're like obviously  like reveling in their monstrosity i  guess honestly i think that's what makes  lim and susie in this book like even  more disturbing they know that they're  monsters they know what they're doing is  bad  but they're so  dispassionate they're kind of killing  more in a hey wouldn't it be interesting  if we ran this woman over like after we  steal her car like for no reason really  they didn't have to they were just  interested or mainly susie was just  interested to see what would happen and  then like after they do it she's just  kind of like huh and then that's kind of  like all she thinks about it like she  doesn't really think about it any 