ine it and you know her inner  thoughts since the narrative is told  from her perspective you can actually  sympathize with her to some degree and  you can also despair  at how  her past actually ended up shaping  these thought and behavior patterns  to the detriment of everyone around her  like it made like society made her a  monster and she seems like kind of aware  of it but she also doesn't really seem  to  know any other way to be because that's  the only thing that she's ever  experienced like i said  and also i think it's tragic too because  she does seem to have some very very  embryonic notions of  human empathy like she's not a complete  cold-blooded monster like she does seem  to have twinges of guilt or twinges of  maybe i shouldn't do that or something  like that so you do actually as it goes  on you do kind of start to root for her  like to you know kind of hope that  she'll be able to overcome  this corrupted nature you know what i  mean but  i you know the book doesn't really seem  to pull any punches in that regard it  seems to imply  that it's maybe too late for her um that  maybe she's too far gone  like to be redeemed which i kind of feel  like makes the story not only  frightening and but it's also like kind  of heartbreaking as well  um so i'm gonna say if you like the work  of jack ketchum in particular and maybe  are looking for something that uh is  again not not a fun time but something  that kind of delves into some really  dark corners  concerning the larger consequences of  trauma  and  literally puts you into the very  unpleasant headspace of a sociopath  without any kind of sentimentality or  embellishment or glamorization or  anything like that then i would  definitely recommend true crime it's a  pretty stunning uh debut novella i have  to say and uh the author this is  definitely uh someone that i'll be  keeping an eye on in the future so if  you've read it um let me know what you  thought about it in the comments and  that will do